// What the build is targeting, as types a program can match on.
//
// These are ordinary enums. The compiler does not know them — it generates
// `target.nest` beside this file naming one variant of each, and that generated
// file is part of `core`, so the only name coupling is between two files of the
// same package rather than between the compiler and a library's vocabulary.
//
// They are enums rather than strings because the whole point of asking is to
// branch: `match OS { Os.Windows => …, _ => … }` is exhaustively checked, and a
// string comparison against `"widnows"` is not.
//
// A variant's name is the setting's name with each `_`-separated word
// capitalized — `x86_64` is `X86_64`, `macos` is `Macos`. That one rule is what
// the generator applies, so adding a target means adding a variant here and a
// spelling to the compiler's list, with nothing in between to keep in step.

// The operating system, or `Bare` for a freestanding target with none.
@public Os :: enum {
  Linux,
  Macos,
  Windows,
  Freebsd,
  Bare,
}

// The processor architecture.
@public Arch :: enum {
  X86_64,
  Aarch64,
  Riscv64,
  Wasm32,
}

// The build profile's name.
//
// The compiler has no opinion about what a profile *means* — whether a release
// build wraps on overflow is the build tool's decision, handed to `nestc` as a
// resolved setting (§ options). This is only the name, carried so a program can
// read it.
@public Profile :: enum {
  Debug,
  Release,
}
